@node	0.3, 0.4, 0.2, Chapter 0
@section Recursion and the call stack

This section covers recursive and iterative processes in this language, and
how tail calls or loops express them. It is the grounding for @ref{1.2},
where the book first separates a recursive process from an iterative one,
and for @ref{3.5}, whose streams defer work this section defers in miniature.

@heading Recursion takes a stack

A plain recursive call takes a stack frame and holds it until the calls it
spawned return. The tree-recursive @code{fib} of @ref{1.2.2} fans out
exactly this way:

@example kotlin
public fun factorial(n: Long): Long =
    if (n <= 1L) 1L else n * factorial(n - 1L)

public fun fib(n: Int): Long =
    if (n < 2) n.toLong() else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

factorial(20)
// => 2432902008176640000

fib(10)
// => 55
@end example

@noindent
Kotlin gives the book's processes no automatic tail-call optimization. A
linear-recursive @code{factorial} written as above is a linear-recursive
process here too, and the JVM's stack bounds how deep it may run.

@heading @code{tailrec} makes the loop

The iterative process of @ref{1.2.1} is expressed with @code{tailrec}: the
compiler checks that every call is a tail call, rewrites the self-call into
a loop, and refuses to compile a function whose self-calls are not
actually in tail position:

@example kotlin
public tailrec fun factorialIter(n: Long,
    acc: Long = 1L): Long =
    if (n <= 1L) acc else factorialIter(n - 1L, acc * n)

factorialIter(20)
// => 2432902008176640000
@end example

@noindent
A @code{while} states the loop directly when no recursion is wanted, and it
carries its state in local variables:

@example kotlin
public fun sumTo(n: Long): Long @{
    var total = 0L
    var k = 1L
    while (k <= n) @{
        total += k
        k += 1
    @}
    return total
@}

sumTo(100)
// => 5050
@end example

@noindent
Two limits shape the rest of the book. A @code{tailrec} function can only
rewrite a call to itself, so two mutually tail-recursive functions cannot
be expressed as such; the evaluators of @ref{4.1} are built as one
dispatch loop for exactly this reason. And deep recursion that is not
tail recursion, such as the tree walks of @ref{1.2} and @ref{2.2.2}, uses
@code{DeepRecursiveFunction}, which keeps the frame state on the heap, when
the default stack would not suffice.

@heading Deferring work: @code{lazy} and @code{Sequence}

A @code{lazy} value records how to compute itself and runs that work at
most once, at the first read. Every later read returns the memoized result:

@example kotlin
public var lazyCalls: Int = 0

public val expensiveAnswer: Long by lazy @{
    lazyCalls += 1
    40L + 2L
@}

expensiveAnswer
// => 42

expensiveAnswer
// => 42

lazyCalls
// => 1
@end example

@noindent
A @code{Sequence} is a cold, composable pipeline. @code{generateSequence}
builds one from a step function, the @code{sequence @{ @}} builder writes a
generator as a suspended loop, and intermediate steps such as @code{map}
and @code{filter} record work without doing any of it. A terminal operation
such as @code{take(n)} or @code{toList()} drives the pipeline:

@example kotlin
public fun naturals(): Sequence<Long> =
    generateSequence(1L) @{ it + 1L @}

public fun squaresSeq(): Sequence<Long> =
    naturals().map @{ x -> x * x @}

public fun evens(): Sequence<Long> =
    sequence @{
        var n = 0L
        while (true) @{
            yield(n)
            n += 2L
        @}
    @}

squaresSeq().take(5).toList()
// => [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]

evens().take(3).toList()
// => [0, 2, 4]
@end example

@noindent
Cold means recomputing: a sequence holds a recipe, not a row of results, so
walking it a second time runs the recipe again from the start. Exercise 0.5
below makes that measurable.

@heading Where @code{Sequence} is not enough

The streams of @ref{3.5} need two properties a @code{Sequence} does not
have. They are memoized, so two cursors over one stream force each element
once; and they permit self-reference, where a tail must capture the very
stream being defined. The runtime therefore ships a second type,
@code{LStream}, the memoized node pair of @ref{3.5.1}: a computed head and
a tail that computes at most once.

@example kotlin
public fun squareStream(n: Long): LStream<Long> =
    consStream(n * n) @{ squareStream(n + 1L) @}

squareStream(1L).take(5)
// => [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
@end example

@noindent
The rule this book follows from @ref{3.5} onward: a @code{Sequence} carries
a pipeline whose elements are consumed as they are produced, and an
@code{LStream} carries a shared, memoized structure that may observe and
reuse its own past.

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.5}Exercise 0.5:} Build the infinite
@code{Sequence} of squares and the memoized @code{LStream} of squares, each
taking a @code{force: () -> Unit} callback that the pipeline calls once per
element computation. Then write a probe that builds one of each, walks each
one twice with @code{take(5)}, and reports the two force counts. The
sequence should charge the second walk in full; the stream should charge it
nothing. The runtime's @code{take(n)} forces one tail beyond the
@code{n}-th head, and make your expected stream count state that.
